    Based on the search results, I found the paper you're looking for. The paper "Lower Bounds for Levin–Kolmogorov Complexity" by Brandt was published at TCC 2024.
    
    The paper develops a novel yet simple diagonalization technique to show unconditionally that MKtP ∉ DTIME[O(n)], meaning no deterministic linear-time algorithm can solve MKtP on every instance. 
    
    If the underlying computational model has a linear-time universal simulation, e.g. random-access machines, then the paper obtains a quadratic lower bound, i.e., MKtP ∉ DTIME[O(n²)].
    
    The paper makes significant progress toward proving that MKtP ∉ P by establishing these unconditional lower bounds using a diagonalization approach rather than relying on circuit lower bounds. You can access the full paper at the Springer link (https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-031-78011-0_7) or the IACR eprint archive (https://eprint.iacr.org/2024/687).
    
    
